<div class="container">
	<div class="subpage-inner">
		<h1>
			<strong>Hosts on national TV used technology I created to present live election results</strong>
		</h1>
		<p class="meta">2015, 2017, 2019, 2021, 2022</p>
		<p>As part of my software engineering role at the Danish National Broadcasting Corporation, I’ve played a vital role in <strong>creating technology used for communicating election results effectively</strong> on election night TV shows. I’ve done this over several years, with varying areas of responsibility.</p>
		<p>When you work on such special projects that have such a sense of urgency, team spirit, and immediacy, a lot of interesting things happen in the process. <strong>Here’s some photos</strong> that show some of the things I’ve been through as part of this role.</p>
		<p>As you look through the photos, you can see how my role starts small, evolves to fill much more, and transitions to a senior role, mostly directing instead of coding.</p>
	</div>
</div>
<div class="content-segment">
	<div class="container">
		<div class="section">
			<div class="section-head">
				<h2>2015</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="section-body">
				<p>The first election night TV show project I was a part of was an EU referendum election in 2015. In the TV studio, located on a wide beautiful hallway in the Danish national legislature building, there was a big glass floor which contained a changing virtual graphics setup, capable of various charts and visualizations.</p>
				<p>I was in charge of the interface the host used to control this crucial part of the show, helping the host understand and manipulate the nuances while on air. I played a big part in its development, and during the broadcast, I literally stood behind the host inside the studio — in a very subtle position — always ready to help out if any issues should occur.</p>
				<figure class="main-photo">
					<img src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2015-12-03 21.24.21.jpg" />
				</figure>
				<figure class="main-photo">
					<img src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2015-12-04 18.53.47.webp" />
					<figcaption><strong class="figcaption-prefix">Above:</strong> That’s me on the left, back there!</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure class="main-photo">
					<img src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2015-12-02 12.15.00.jpg" />
					<figcaption><strong class="figcaption-prefix">Above:</strong> The host interface I helped build. It showed the results in a big font and allowed the host to put the results front and center in the studio using large, easy to access buttons.</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure class="main-photo main-photo-portrait">
					<img src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2015-12-01 16.48.54.jpg" />
					<figcaption><strong class="figcaption-prefix">Above:</strong> For the first election night TV show project I participated in, I developed from scratch a system to show, in real time, all the newest election results from the backend system, on a producer’s screen, behind the scenes. The system allowed the producer to pick out results and send them to the host’s system.</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure class="main-photo">
					<div class="photo-sequence">
