				</figure>
				<p>One of the more fun stories I look back on happened during the preparation of this show: After I had developed my system to display results on TV, being built in Node.js and React, it had to be hosted somewhere.</p>
				<p>We never found a great place, as all the servers used at the company at the time were Windows machines, all of which were not accustomed to newer Node.js technology. In the end, we ended up hosting my system on my own laptop! Yep, the one that was with me, directly behind the hosts inside the studio.</p>
				<p>Because of this, as seen below, on the day before the election, I went to go get an ethernet adapter for my laptop for a stable connection! I, um… certainly wouldn’t do that again, but everything worked just fine!</p>
				<figure class="main-photo main-photo-portrait">
					<figcaption><strong class="figcaption-prefix">Below:</strong> “Important production equipment for election night on national TV”</figcaption>
					<img loading="lazy" src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2015-12-03 01.08.04.jpg" />
				</figure>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>
<div class="content-segment">
	<div class="container">
		<div class="section">
			<div class="section-head">
				<h2>2017</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="section-body">
				<p>Fast forward to 2017, where Denmark held its local elections. I once again was in charge of the same systems, this time refining them and adapting them to show local results, including a bunch of miscellaneous local parties. I was working together with the same host, and was sitting close by for assistance on election night. This time, the studio was in the fancy modern main building of the network.</p>
				<figure class="main-photo">
					<!-- <img loading="lazy" src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2017-11-21 16.02.07.jpg" /> -->
					<img class="main-photo-portrait" src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2017-11-19 15.34.14.jpg" />
					<figcaption><strong class="figcaption-prefix">Above:</strong> The graphics worked mostly the same as in 2015, with the addition of a big circular screen, showing a map of Denmark laid flat.</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure class="main-photo main-photo-portrait">
					<img loading="lazy" src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2017-11-20 22.34.48.jpg" />
					<figcaption><strong class="figcaption-prefix">Above:</strong> Hand written instructions for the host written the night before the show.</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure class="main-photo">
					<img loading="lazy" src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2017-11-21 15.28.09.jpg" />
					<figcaption><strong class="figcaption-prefix">Above:</strong> My desk was near the host once again. I was often on TV in the background, much to my mom’s delight.</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<p>I don’t have a picture for this story, but for this election, I also played a role in the morning wake-up election news show the morning after the election. Yes, that meant something like 3–4 hours of sleep that night.</p>
				<p>I don’t remember why, but a few minutes before the morning show was set to go live, we had to restart parts of the setup. Having restarted my system moments before the time to go live, the host’s computer showed an error message. <strong>Literally 30 seconds before we were on TV,</strong> he looked back at me with semi-panic on his face. I went up to the stage in a smooth motion, hit the F5 button on the keyboard, and everything was fine, and the TV host was relaxed again. I went back to my desk just behind me, my heart beating a little faster than usual — even though I felt like a super badass tech ninja.</p>
				<p>The rest of that morning was (thankfully) very uneventful for me.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>
<div class="content-segment">
	<div class="container">
		<div class="section">
			<div class="section-head">
				<h2>2019</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="section-body">
				<p>This was a big year. Not just because it contained two elections very close to each other (European parliament election followed by the national parliament election), but because I moved from working on behind-the-scenes products that were merely <em>controlling</em> the TV graphics, onward to <em>implementing the TV graphics themselves</em> in JavaScript! That’s right! <strong>JavaScript on TV!</strong>
				</p>
				<p>Clearly I was representing a technology that was on the rise, but I also took it as a signal that the network had massive confidence in my work.</p>
				<p>Additionally, we were lucky to work with very cutting edge hardware: A transparent LCD screen, borrowed directly from the Korean tech giant LG, who had representatives come over and deliver it to us.</p>
				<p>They were very fascinated with our use case: Because the transparent screen showed content only to one side, but looked like a fully see-through window on the other side, we could have a TV camera positioned behind the screen, with the host on the other side. The host could then use the LCD screen to manipulate TV graphics with his fingers, as if he was <em>touching the viewer’s TV screens and using it like an iPad</em>. Mind(s) blown!</p>
				<p>It was a great project and exciting vision that came to fruition thanks to the hard work of many talented people from many different fields working together.</p>
				<figure class="main-photo">
					<img loading="lazy" src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2019 IMG_1850.jpeg" />
					<figcaption><strong class="figcaption-prefix">Above:</strong> The result, as it looked from my desk behind the scenes on election night. The larger monitor shows what the viewers would see; the host interacting directly with the election results by seemingly manipulating objects on the viewer’s TV screens. In reality, he was touching a transparent LCD screen showing a browser window containing my project. My project was then mirroring his interactions to a separate output, which was superimposed on the TV signal, and the result looked seamless.</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure class="main-photo">
					<img loading="lazy" src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2019 IMG_1669.jpeg" />
					<figcaption><strong class="figcaption-prefix">Above:</strong> What the hardware setup looked like in its early stages. The superimposed output, which is a separate output compared to what’s on the controlling screen, is shown on the right. Notice how the controlling screen contains an output which is mirrored. In effect, it is like looking at a mirrored version of the TV output. However, the numbers are still facing the correct direction, and are perfectly readable.</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<p>Because the unique setup meant that the host had to work with a mirrored map of the country, I created a mirrored map in print form, with all the district names, for the host to prepare for the show. I got word that he was using it nearly every day leading up to the election, and that it took a while to get used to — especially the fact that local western districts were on the right instead of the left.</p>
				<p>It can seem like a silly thing for a software developer to spend time on, but this is one of my favorite examples of something I did outside of my role that surely made a big difference, even though it wasn’t related to my core responsibilities.</p>
				<p>I wanted to do all I could to make sure the project succeeded! And to that end, doing everything to prepare the one person who’s going to use your product in front of millions of viewers is very important. And seeing as I already had the correct SVG shapes with the correct colors and labels, it was relatively easy to pop that into Illustrator and finish it up.</p>
				<figure class="main-photo">
					<figcaption><strong class="figcaption-prefix">Below:</strong> The mirrored map print I made for preparation purposes</figcaption>
					<img loading="lazy" src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2019 IMG_1688.jpeg" />
				</figure>
				<figure class="main-photo">
					<img loading="lazy" src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2019 IMG_1736.jpeg" />
					<figcaption><strong class="figcaption-prefix">Above:</strong> The translucent LCD display in the studio, from behind, in front of the fixed camera position. Notice the raw-looking inner cables visible. This really was an LG prototype that we were privileged to work with. My hardware colleague is visible taping down the setup; he did such a great job making this project come to life.</figcaption>
				</figure>
