					<img loading="lazy" src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2019 IMG_1847.jpeg" />
					<figcaption><strong class="figcaption-prefix">Above:</strong> The director general of the Danish National Broadcasting Corporation comes by the day before election night to witness the setup.</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure class="main-photo">
					<img loading="lazy" src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2019 IMG_1724.jpeg" />
					<figcaption><strong class="figcaption-prefix">Above:</strong> Additionally, the TV studio contained a set of vertical LED displays functioning as large bar charts. I was also responsible for producing the graphical output for these. My output consisted of a page in my application which rendered bar charts on specific pixel values. These were then mapped by the hardware folks to these grand displays. It was another — pretty massive — way the host could communicate the results.</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure class="main-photo">
					<img loading="lazy" src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2019-IMG_1877-resized.jpg" />
					<figcaption><strong class="figcaption-prefix">Above:</strong> The raw output of the browser page which was shown on the LEDs. These two computers behind the scenes had the sole role of showing these browser pages without interruption.</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure class="main-photo">
					<img loading="lazy" src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2019 IMG_1769.jpeg" />
					<figcaption><strong class="figcaption-prefix">Above:</strong> Not only was the data output being sent to the bar chart displays updating in real time as the total votes across the country were tabulated. I was also tasked with building a manually operated <em>reveal mechanism</em> for the exit poll, which was to be presented right as the polls were closing. This allowed the host to step through each party, one-by-one, in the revealing of the exit poll. The stepping interface was controlled by a producer in the production room.</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure class="main-photo">
					<img loading="lazy" src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2019 IMG_1874.jpeg" />
					<figcaption><strong class="figcaption-prefix">Above:</strong> My desk during the election show was very close to both of these big products. I also had the ability to talk to, and receive word from, the producer’s desk using the intercom.</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<p>In the midst of all this, I was also still responsible for the election results interface used by producers in the production room, preparing results and sending them to the host. This tool was mostly in maintenance mode, being the same as in previous years. Although it did have to be refactored a little to work with the data for parliament elections.</p>
				<p>I remember walking back and forth between the studio and the production room that night, helping everyone if they had an issue of any kind. That suits me just fine, as communicating technical things to non-technical people — making technology work for people! — is one of my passion areas.</p>
				<figure class="main-photo">
					<figcaption><strong class="figcaption-prefix">Below:</strong> Producer seen using my interface in the foreground, in the production room.</figcaption>
					<img loading="lazy" src="/assets/election-photos/raw/2019 IMG_1786.jpeg" />
				</figure>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>
<div class="content-segment">
